        "Attributes": [
          {
            "Id": 758501,
            "attributeType": "LongString",
            "attributeName": "AIDescription",
            "attributeRelationType": "None",
            "Value": "The TurboGrafx-16, known as the PC Engine in Japan, was a pioneering fourth-generation home video game console developed by Hudson Soft and manufactured by NEC. Released in Japan in 1987 and North America in 1989, it was the first console of its generation, initially competing with the Nintendo Family Computer. While a commercial success in Japan, its delayed North American debut hindered its performance against the Sega Genesis and Super Nintendo. \n\nFeaturing an 8-bit CPU paired with dual 16-bit graphics processors, the TurboGrafx-16 boasted impressive visuals and supported a palette of 512 colors. Popular games included *Bonk's Adventure*, *R-Type*, *Ys I & II*, and conversions of arcade hits like *Galaga '88* and *Splatterhouse*.  The system utilized HuCards for game distribution, with later expansions including CD-ROM and LaserDisc formats. Despite its innovations, the TurboGrafx-16 ultimately faded, succeeded by the PC-FX."
          },
